\section{Choosing a covariance estimator}{

Let each independent unit contribute a score vector, let \code{B} be the sum of
their outer products, and let \code{A} be the observed information. Independent
units are people in a panel model and rows in a cross-sectional model.\tabular{lll}{
   Choice \tab Calculation \tab Common use and computation \cr
   \code{"opg"} \tab Inverse of \code{B} \tab Default likelihood-based covariance; uses the stored unit scores and needs no Hessian. \cr
   \code{"observed"} \tab Inverse of \code{A} \tab Curvature-based likelihood inference; computes information if it is not already stored. \cr
   \code{"sandwich"} \tab \code{solve(A) \%*\% B \%*\% solve(A)} \tab Independent-unit sandwich inference; also requires valid observed information. It does not correct an inaccurate likelihood integral or establish model identification. \cr
   Named matrix \tab Supplied natural covariance \tab Available to \code{confint()}, \code{\link[=summary]{summary()}}, and \code{\link[=predict]{predict()}}, for externally calculated uncertainty. See \code{\link[=predict.panelselect]{predict.panelselect()}} for matrix validation. \cr
}


Assign \code{fit = add_information(fit)} to retain information for repeated
